# Direction (RTL / LTR)

`document.documentElement.dir` follows the active Wikipedia edition. The
**world** (terrain, portals in world space, camera) never flips. **UI chrome**
mirrors with reading direction.

## Default: logical CSS

Prefer `inset-inline-*`, `padding-inline-*`, `margin-inline-*`,
`border-inline-*`, `text-align: start|end`. Flex/grid already follow `dir`.

## Physical axes are rare — and must be labeled

Use physical `left` / `right` / `translateX(±50%)` only when needed:

1. **Centering** with `left: 50%` + `translate(-50%, …)` — both axes must be
   physical. Never pair `inset-inline-start: 50%` with physical translate
   (under `dir=rtl` that pins the start edge mid-viewport then shoves the
   box further left, off-screen). Prefer flex/`place-items: center` when
   practical.
2. **Screen-space JS** (projected tooltips, portal preview cards, legend
   pins) — coordinates are viewport pixels.
3. **Safe-area `env()`** — notches are physical; pair logical inset with a
   `[dir='rtl']` swap of `safe-area-inset-left/right` when docking to an edge.
4. **Directional icons** — `scaleX(-1)` under `[dir='rtl']` for chevrons only,
   not for layout containers or the world.

When you write a physical inset or translate for UI, leave a short comment
naming which exception above applies.

## Text

Visible chrome: `<bdi>` / `I18nText`. Field Guide prose sets `:dir="uiDir"`
on the panel so mixed Latin+Hebrew inherits the locale direction.
